{"results": [{"id": "hp_doc_e785d86c43160ef7bff95dfdf8956e6f9caa82c881aaf6cd595a9f107fa9a919", "title": "MG 30", "text": "The \"Maschinengewehr\" 30, or MG 30 was a German-designed machine gun that saw some service with various armed forces in the 1930s. It was also modified to become the standard German aircraft gun as the MG 15 and MG 17. It is most notable as the design pattern that led to the MG 34 and MG 42, and thus is one of the major ancestors of many of the weapons in service which would later find widespread use into the 21st century.", "score": 24.507}, {"id": "hp_doc_a84179246c4e92e7dc3c4a0221231c0e3d202ec3986b1960a618ec4732f29d0f", "title": "Null object pattern", "text": "In object-oriented computer programming, a Null Object is an object with no referenced value or with defined neutral (\"null\") behavior. The Null Object design pattern describes the uses of such objects and their behavior (or lack thereof). It was first published in the \"Pattern Languages of Program Design\" book series.", "score": 18.3281}, {"id": "hp_doc_fbf8953538e00201f1b4815b6364de53c950a2cee50a83228368c6b9b336db6e", "title": "PatternShare", "text": "PatternShare was a computer software design pattern web resource, hosted by Microsoft. Much of the work was led by Ward Cunningham, of Microsoft. In 2007 Pattern Share was taken offline.", "score": 17.0489}], "remaining_searches": 2}
Choose your next action.

