{"results": [{"id": "hp_doc_4b2683d0b7ee85b0d49f8c2804517049736b75e936375d054848b592f9158ff8", "title": "MG 34", "text": "The \"Maschinengewehr\" 34, or MG 34, is a German recoil-operated air-cooled machine gun, first tested in 1929, introduced in 1934, and issued to units in 1936. It accepts the 7.92×57mm Mauser cartridge, and is generally considered the world's first general-purpose machine gun.", "score": 34.7253}, {"id": "hp_doc_e785d86c43160ef7bff95dfdf8956e6f9caa82c881aaf6cd595a9f107fa9a919", "title": "MG 30", "text": "The \"Maschinengewehr\" 30, or MG 30 was a German-designed machine gun that saw some service with various armed forces in the 1930s. It was also modified to become the standard German aircraft gun as the MG 15 and MG 17. It is most notable as the design pattern that led to the MG 34 and MG 42, and thus is one of the major ancestors of many of the weapons in service which would later find widespread use into the 21st century.", "score": 19.5136}, {"id": "hp_doc_860f50019383c6491e395592e38bd573ee60e925e13d7a907749beaa80ad9293", "title": "Marlyn Meltzer", "text": "Marlyn Wescoff Meltzer (1922 – December 4, 2008) was one of the six original programmers of ENIAC, the first general-purpose electronic digital computer.", "score": 14.4999}], "remaining_searches": 1}
